{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap for `requirements.sdk.graph.snapshot`.\n\n**Obligations, and the tests that cover them**\n- **A node read builds a `Snapshot` from `Domain::node_content` and its `NodeContents::fingerprint`.**\n  - `direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots` (`crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs`) calls `read_snapshot` on empty and large multi-byte contents. It asserts that `snapshot.content.format()` equals the original and that `snapshot.fingerprint` equals `fp(original)`.\n  - `snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions` (same file) checks the `read_snapshot` fingerprint against `fp(\"before\")`. It then changes the stored content and checks that the earlier snapshot and recorded dependencies keep the \"before\" fingerprint.\n  - `identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot` (`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs`) asserts `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint`, and that `snapshot.fingerprint.value` is \"before\" after the underlying content changes.\n  - The other marked tests (`explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots`, the two agent tests in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs`, and `requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk` in `src/sdk.rs`) also check fingerprints from snapshot reads, but they are not needed for adequacy.\n- **\"Without another read\".** The `Memory` graph's `reads` log is asserted exactly: `[1, 1]` in the Unicode test and `[1]` in the contracts test. Each `read_snapshot` or `read` costs one `node_content` call, and fingerprinting adds none.\n- **`Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)` and the public fields.** `callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint` (`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs`) builds a snapshot through `new` from an integration-test crate. It asserts both `content` and `fingerprint`.\n- **`#[non_exhaustive]`, the `Clone` and `Debug` impls, and the exact struct and `new` signatures.** `public_exports_match_the_appendix` (`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs`) drives `check_declaration`. That function compares the rustdoc trait-implementation set against the listed impls, which are `Clone` and `Debug` here. It also asserts that structs with public fields are `#[non_exhaustive]`, and it compares declarations and inherent members.\n- The implementation in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]` has `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]` and `#[non_exhaustive]`. No test calls `Snapshot::clone` or formats a snapshot with `Debug`. For derived impls that is acceptable, because the API-inventory test verifies the impls exist and a derive has no logic of its own.\n\n**Delegated or out of scope**\n- The fingerprint's content semantics belong to the cited `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, so I did not reassess them.\n- The edge-read snapshots (`outgoing_edges`) are outside this requirement's \"node reads\" scope.",
